    // Your implementation here
}
```

Example:
```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

struct Rule {
    // Define the rule structure
}

type Id = u32;
type Item = u32;

fn process_rules(ruleset: &HashMap<Id, Rule>, id: Id, next: Vec<Item>) -> Vec<Item> {
    // Your implementation here
}

fn main() {
    let ruleset: HashMap<Id, Rule> = HashMap::new();
    let id: Id = 1;
    let next: Vec<Item> = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    let result = process_rules(&ruleset, id, next);
    println!("{:?}", result);
}
```

### Solution:
```rust
fn process_rules(ruleset: &HashMap<Id, Rule>, id: Id, next: Vec<Item>) -> Vec<Item> {
    let rule = ruleset.get(&id).unwrap(); // Retrieve the rule based on the given ID
    let next_next = next
        .iter()
        .map(|remain| rule.matches(ruleset, remain)) // Apply the rule to each item in the next collection
        .filter(|v| !v.is_empty()) // Filter out empty results
        .flatten() // Flatten the nested collections
        .collect(); // Collect the matched items into a new collection
    next_next // Return the resulting collection of matched items
}
```

In the solution, the `process_rules` function retrieves the rule based on the given ID from the `ruleset`. It then applies the rule to each item in the `next` collection using the `rule.matches` function. The results are filtered to remove empty collections, flattened into a single collection, and returned as the resulting matched items.

